Bundle-Name: Eclipse Fennec Model Atlas Publisher
Bundle-Description: Writing to a model.atlas from code: ObjectPublisher stores one object in a \
 configured object registry, PackagePublisher hands a registered EPackage to a configured schema \
 stage. Both are configured services — the scope, the registry, the stage and whether an existing \
 entry may be replaced belong to the deployment, never to the caller.

# Deploying this bundle and configuring a factory PID IS the authorization decision:
# a runtime holding neither configuration cannot write to an atlas from code, which
# is why the widely consumed, read-only ModelAtlasClient still has no write on it.
# What is exported is the two contracts and their receipts — strings and ints, no
# ObjectMetadata, no Promise. The transport stays inside: HTTP goes through the
# ResourceSet's URIConverter (Fennec's RESTful URI handler, already in every Fennec
# runtime) rather than a JAX-RS client, so there is no Jersey stack to deploy and no
# provider lookup that can lose a race with lazy bundle activation.

-workingset: atlas

-library: enableEMF

-buildpath: \
	org.eclipse.fennec.emf.osgi.metadata;version=latest,\
	org.eclipse.fennec.emf.osgi.api;version=latest,\
	org.osgi.service.metatype.annotations;version=latest

# component.minimal exports org.eclipse.fennec.emf.osgi.fingerprint.util, whose
# FingerprintHelper backs MetadataServices.createWhiteboard(). The tests resolve
# packages through a real metadata whiteboard rather than a stub, so that
# "registered" means here what it means in the runtime.
-testpath: \
	assertj-core,\
	org.eclipse.fennec.emf.osgi.component.minimal;version=latest
